{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019TyJVuokbN4ZgxL2p9TPQ5","type":"tool_result","content":"353:[Omitted long matching line]\n500:**4.** `apiVersion` selects which version of the Kubernetes API you are using to create this object [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23]: effectively, which schema the rest of the document should be parsed under. It is per-object rather than per-file because a single file can contain several objects of different kinds, and different resource types are versioned independently. A Pod and a NetworkPolicy in one file do not share a version, and there is no sensible file-level answer.\n835:[Omitted long matching line]\n837:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** Once you internalize that all of those are the same mechanism pointed at different things, four chapters get considerably easier. ReplicaSet, Service, NetworkPolicy, and node affinity are not four mechanisms to learn. They are one mechanism, *describe a set by its attributes*, aimed at four problems. Learn the primitive once and you spend the later chapters learning what each resource *does* with its set, which is the interesting part."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63f373ff-86db-43bd-953d-659db0acabb5","uuid":"7551e851-66fc-49d4-9c5b-2a9cb6516789","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:14:36.968Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"353:[Omitted long matching line]\n500:**4.** `apiVersion` selects which version of the Kubernetes API you are using to create this object [source: k8s-docs-objects-2026-08-23]: effectively, which schema the rest of the document should be parsed under. It is per-object rather than per-file because a single file can contain several objects of different kinds, and different resource types are versioned independently. A Pod and a NetworkPolicy in one file do not share a version, and there is no sensible file-level answer.\n835:[Omitted long matching line]\n837:> ⚓ **Worth Securing:** Once you internalize that all of those are the same mechanism pointed at different things, four chapters get considerably easier. ReplicaSet, Service, NetworkPolicy, and node affinity are not four mechanisms to learn. They are one mechanism, *describe a set by its attributes*, aimed at four problems. Learn the primitive once and you spend the later chapters learning what each resource *does* with its set, which is the interesting part.","numLines":4,"totalLines":4}}
