{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WGCfsbjveuWvJxbq5C3kkZ","type":"tool_result","content":"482:**1.** ⚪ `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose a PostgreSQL database and a web application to clients outside the cluster. Which one can an Ingress handle, and what do you use for the other?\n490:**5.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 gave you a one-sentence rule about objects and components. State it, then name the two things from Chapter 9 that were instances of it.\n526:**5. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.** The two Chapter 9 instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision one, and a Service whose selector matches no Pods.\n1074:**1.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch4]` Chapter 4 said a NetworkPolicy selects both its subject and its peers. Point at the two selectors in that sentence and say what each one is choosing.\n1088:**1. `[retrieval: ch4]` The policy's own top-level `podSelector` chooses which Pods the policy applies to. The selectors inside its rules — `podSelector` and `namespaceSelector` under `from`/`to` — choose which Pods and namespaces those Pods may talk to.**\n1256:**2.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose an HTTP application and a message broker speaking its own binary protocol, both to clients outside the cluster. What do you need?\n1291:**7.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 said a controller watches for objects and drives reality toward what they describe. An Ingress controller is one. What does it watch, and what does it change?\n1354:**16.** 🟡 `[retrieval: ch4]` A single NetworkPolicy carries a selector at the top of its `spec` and further selectors underneath `ingress.from`. Which chooses what — and what happens to the policy's effect on a Pod if someone relabels that Pod out from under the top-level selector?\n1368:**18.** 🟡 `[retrieval: ch3]` Name the rule Chapter 3 gave you about objects and components, and every instance of it you have met in this book so far.\n1391:**2. B.** `[retrieval: ch9]`\n1559:**16. A.** `[retrieval: ch4]`\n1587:**18. A.** `[retrieval: ch3]`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63f373ff-86db-43bd-953d-659db0acabb5","uuid":"75c27767-f804-4406-802e-fd260dd0c0b0","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:15:38.277Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"482:**1.** ⚪ `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose a PostgreSQL database and a web application to clients outside the cluster. Which one can an Ingress handle, and what do you use for the other?\n490:**5.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 gave you a one-sentence rule about objects and components. State it, then name the two things from Chapter 9 that were instances of it.\n526:**5. `[retrieval: ch3]` An object without its component does nothing.** The two Chapter 9 instances: a `type: LoadBalancer` Service on a cluster with no load balancer to provision one, and a Service whose selector matches no Pods.\n1074:**1.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch4]` Chapter 4 said a NetworkPolicy selects both its subject and its peers. Point at the two selectors in that sentence and say what each one is choosing.\n1088:**1. `[retrieval: ch4]` The policy's own top-level `podSelector` chooses which Pods the policy applies to. The selectors inside its rules — `podSelector` and `namespaceSelector` under `from`/`to` — choose which Pods and namespaces those Pods may talk to.**\n1256:**2.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch9]` You need to expose an HTTP application and a message broker speaking its own binary protocol, both to clients outside the cluster. What do you need?\n1291:**7.** 🔵 `[retrieval: ch3]` Chapter 3 said a controller watches for objects and drives reality toward what they describe. An Ingress controller is one. What does it watch, and what does it change?\n1354:**16.** 🟡 `[retrieval: ch4]` A single NetworkPolicy carries a selector at the top of its `spec` and further selectors underneath `ingress.from`. Which chooses what — and what happens