{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UrdQwgntiYQtanrSYEtqhq","type":"tool_result","content":"If you do not specify an IngressClass on an Ingress and your cluster has **exactly one** IngressClass marked as default, Kubernetes applies that default [source: k8s-docs-ingress-controllers-2026-08-24]. You mark one as default by setting the `ingressclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class` annotation to the string `\"true\nThat is the documentation's own phrasing, twice over: the Ingress page and the Ingress Controllers page say the same thing in nearly the same words [source: k8s-docs-ingress-controllers-2026-08-24]. It matters because the promise of a portable object is undercut, precisely, by the gap between a reference specification \n<!-- FIGURE: ch10-fig04-networkpolicy-additive-selectors -->\nWhen a NetworkPolicy does nothing, **traffic flows exactly as it did before.** `kubectl get networkpolicy` shows the object. `kubectl describe` shows the rules, correctly parsed and neatly formatted. Everything you can observe about the object says it is fine, and the observable behaviour of an unenforced policy is *id","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63f373ff-86db-43bd-953d-659db0acabb5","uuid":"5a47a000-9f06-45da-b800-187125e80a11","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:15:53.549Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"If you do not specify an IngressClass on an Ingress and your cluster has **exactly one** IngressClass marked as default, Kubernetes applies that default [source: k8s-docs-ingress-controllers-2026-08-24]. You mark one as default by setting the `ingressclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class` annotation to the string `\"true\nThat is the documentation's own phrasing, twice over: the Ingress page and the Ingress Controllers page say the same thing in nearly the same words [source: k8s-docs-ingress-controllers-2026-08-24]. It matters because the promise of a portable object is undercut, precisely, by the gap between a reference specification \n<!-- FIGURE: ch10-fig04-networkpolicy-additive-selectors -->\nWhen a NetworkPolicy does nothing, **traffic flows exactly as it did before.** `kubectl get networkpolicy` shows the object. `kubectl describe` shows the rules, correctly parsed and neatly formatted. Everything you can observe about the object says it is fine, and the observable behaviour of an unenforced policy is *id","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
