{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DMi3X94KTcn12SFnD9hgUW","type":"tool_result","content":"363:> 🪝 **Snag:** Each container in a Pod does **not** get its own IP address. The Pod gets one; all its containers share it. This is the easiest carry-over error to make from single-container Docker experience, where \"one container, one IP\" was a safe assumption. In Kubernetes it is not.\n830: readiness   │ Can the container       │ Pod IP REMOVED from  │ kill or restart\n1123:| \"Each container in a Pod gets its own IP\" | The **Pod** gets one IP, shared by all its containers. |\n1445:| **Readiness probe** | Fails → Pod IP removed from matching Services' endpoints. Nothing is killed. |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"63f373ff-86db-43bd-953d-659db0acabb5","uuid":"bdc82e7b-fde6-47c4-b8de-fc513aeab082","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:17:01.773Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"363:> 🪝 **Snag:** Each container in a Pod does **not** get its own IP address. The Pod gets one; all its containers share it. This is the easiest carry-over error to make from single-container Docker experience, where \"one container, one IP\" was a safe assumption. In Kubernetes it is not.\n830: readiness   │ Can the container       │ Pod IP REMOVED from  │ kill or restart\n1123:| \"Each container in a Pod gets its own IP\" | The **Pod** gets one IP, shared by all its containers. |\n1445:| **Readiness probe** | Fails → Pod IP removed from matching Services' endpoints. Nothing is killed. |","numLines":4,"totalLines":4}}
